  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of what Hipparchus invented and contributed to science:\n\n---\n\n## Hipparchus \u2013 Inventions & Contributions\n\nHipparchus is often called the **\"Father of Astronomy\"**. He was a Greek astronomer and mathematician who made fundamental contributions to the advancement of astronomy as a mathematical science and to the foundations of trigonometry.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd0 Trigonometry\nHe made an early contribution to trigonometry by producing a **table of chords**, an early example of a trigonometric table; indeed, some historians go so far as to say that trigonometry was **invented by him**. The purpose of this table of chords was to give a method for solving triangles which avoided solving each triangle from first principles. He also introduced the **division of a circle into 360 degrees** into Greece.\n\n---\n\n### \u2b50 First Star Catalog\nHe is credited with creating the **first reliable star catalog**, which included approximately 850 stars and established a system of **stellar magnitude**. Using a thin tube called a **dioptra** to scan the heavens, Hipparchus made extensive observations of star positions.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf0d Precession of the Equinoxes\nHipparchus **discovered the precession of equinoxes** by comparing his notes with earlier observers; his realization that the points of solstice and equinox moved slowly from east to west against the fixed background of the stars was one of his most significant achievements. He calculated the length of the year to within **6.5 minutes**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf19 Models of the Sun and Moon\nHe was the first Greek to develop **quantitative and accurate models for the motion of the Sun and Moon**, making use of the observations and knowledge accumulated over centuries by the Chaldeans from Babylonia. Based on his solar and lunar theories and his numerical trigonometry, he was probably the first to develop a reliable method to **predict solar eclipses**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2d Astronomical Instruments\nHipparchus is credited with the **invention or improvement of several astronomical instruments**. According to Synesius of Ptolemais, he made the first **astrolabion**, which may have been an armillary sphere or the predecessor of the planar instrument called the **astrolabe**. During his period of observation he may have invented the **planispheric astrolabe**, a device on which the celestial sphere is projected onto the plane of the equator.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f Geography & Coordinates\nHe developed tools for measuring celestial angles and contributed to the systematic use of **geographic coordinates**, pioneering the application of **latitude and longitude**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcbb The Antikythera Mechanism (Possible)\nHe is one of the strongest contenders for the honor of being the **inventor of the Antikythera Mechanism** (also known as the Antikythera Device), considered the world's **first analogue computer**.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, Hipparchus was one of the most prolific and influential scientists of the ancient world, with contributions spanning trigonometry, astronomy, geography, and instrument-making.",
